WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:03.020
INTRO:
Ever since Putin invaded Ukraine, the world

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:08.040
has bombarded Russia with sanctions, restrictions,
and limitations.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:14.331
In terms of transportation, Boeing, Airbus,
Ford, Mercedes, Toyota, and Volkswagen have

00:00:14.331 --> 00:00:18.810
all suspended exports/and or stopped operations
in Russia.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:24.240
In terms of tech, Apple has halted all product
sales, Google has demonetized Russian state

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.159
media, and Facebook and TikTok have blocked
Russian state media altogether in Europe.

00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:35.440
In terms of energy, Exxon, Shell, BP, and
Equinor have announced that they will be canceling

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.010
investments in Russia and/or leaving Russia
completely.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:44.140
In terms of logistics, UPS and FedEx have
halted shipments to Russia and Ukraine.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.671
In terms of finances, several Russian banks
have been barred from the SWIFT system, and

00:00:48.671 --> 00:00:51.429
Switzerland has frozen Russian assets.

00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:55.800
And that doesn’t even include the countrywide
bans on bonds, airspace restrictions, and

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.920
limitations on Russian banks and oligarchs.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.829
Considering all this, there’s no question
that once everything is said and done in Ukraine,

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:05.379
Russia is screwed economically.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:11.630
In fact, in just the past 1 month, the USD
to Ruble exchange rate has exploded from 75

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:15.220
rubles per dollar to over 120 rubles per dollar.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:20.040
This means that the Russian Ruble has lost
nearly 40% of its value against the dollar.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:25.130
Meanwhile, the Russian stock market crashed
33% the day after the invasion even with the

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.570
Russian central bank closing the stock market.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:32.280
And at the time of making this video, Russia
has refused to reopen the market in fear of

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.520
even greater losses.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.869
So, how screwed really is the Russian economy?

00:01:37.869 --> 00:01:44.119
ECONOMIC SPIRAL:
We hear all this news about sanctions and

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:47.950
restrictions on Russia, but what does this
really do to the economy?

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:52.060
Well, from a consumer’s perspective, it
seems like you just can’t travel as much

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.619
abroad, and you can’t buy certain foreign
goods.

00:01:54.619 --> 00:02:00.080
But, the true consequences are much more deep-rooted
and painful with the first being uncontrollable

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.289
inflation.

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:06.390
Like any other product on the market, the
Russian Ruble is subject to supply and demand

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:07.390
constraints.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.860
And the value of the Ruble in relation to
other currencies is dependent on how many

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.950
people want the Ruble vs the other currency.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.590
When a country is exporting a bunch of goods
like Russia, a lot of countries and companies

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.050
will need the Ruble to purchase Russian goods.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:25.390
But, when basically every Russian good is
blocked from being imported, the demand for

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.200
the Ruble falls off a cliff.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.250
In the meantime, Russians are still dependent
on a lot of foreign goods including basics

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.560
like food, clothing, and medicine.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:38.730
And in order to obtain these essentials, Russians
must give up rubles in exchange for dollars,

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.980
euros, or whatever the case may be.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.800
Russia has tried to soften this demand by
limiting imports into the country.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.170
But, with how interconnected the global economy
is today, it’s nearly impossible to break

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.290
away and become independent.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.650
This means that the supply of rubles from
Russians hasn’t really changed all that

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:58.970
much while the demand for the ruble from foreigners
has evaporated.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:03.840
And basic economics would tell you that this
screws over the Ruble, and that’s why it

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.650
has dropped nearly 40% in the past few weeks.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:11.430
But uncontrollable inflation is not even that
bad when you consider that the entire financial

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.060
system is in danger of collapsing.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:19.340
You see, not only do sanctions limit the demand
for rubles, but it makes it extremely difficult

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.820
to access rubles that are stored in foreign
countries.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.400
Ever since 2014, Russia’s central bank has
been building up massive positions in foreign

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.400
currencies.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.530
It’s estimated that they hold $630 billion
in foreign currencies.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:35.902
This was a strategic move to protect Russia
against inflation, but this has kind of backfired.

00:03:35.902 --> 00:03:42.110
The $630 billion worth of foreign currency
is worth significantly more than if they had

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.080
just held that money in rubles.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.050
But, given all these sanctions, they don’t
actually have access to this money.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:53.380
Not only does this make it more difficult
for Russia to fund their activities, but more

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:56.560
importantly, they’re now facing a massive
liquidity problem.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.970
And given that this is public knowledge, Russian
banks are scrambling to prevent a bank run.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:06.280
One of these countermeasures was raising interest
rates from 9.5% to 20%.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.300
This is truly quite amusing.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.971
Over here in the US, the stock market is throwing
a fit because Powell might increase interests

00:04:12.971 --> 00:04:15.950
rates by half a percent after months of heads
up.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:20.650
But, Russia literally raised interest rates
by 10.5% overnight.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.120
Russia is hoping that 20% interest rates will
convince Russians to keep their money in the

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.350
bank and that they can avoid a bank run.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:31.969
But, the situation is still extremely fragile,
and one big catalyst could destroy the Russian

00:04:31.969 --> 00:04:33.970
financial system.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:39.569
INFLATION SPIRAL:
Even if Russia is able to avoid a full-on

00:04:39.569 --> 00:04:44.389
financial collapse, the next several years
will still be quite painful anyway.

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:49.180
You see, when a bunch of everyday goods rise
in price by 40%, people will need to spend

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.889
significantly more money on living expenses.

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:56.790
For many everyday Russians, this means withdrawing
from savings and/or going broke.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:01.219
In the meantime, businesses will have to pay
significantly more for their goods which means

00:05:01.219 --> 00:05:04.509
that they’ll have to raise prices on their
products just to stay afloat.

00:05:04.509 --> 00:05:09.090
But, by raising prices, a lot of their customers
would no longer be able to afford these goods

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:14.020
which means that they’ll get less business
and may end up going bankrupt anyway.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.710
Businesses that do survive will have to pay
their employees significantly higher salaries

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.530
which means that they’ll probably be more
conservative when it comes to hiring, and

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:24.289
they might even let go of a few employees.

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:28.289
Not only does this dramatically increase the
workload for the remaining employees, but

00:05:28.289 --> 00:05:30.969
this will dramatically increase unemployment.

00:05:30.969 --> 00:05:35.249
And when more people are unemployed, consumers
as a whole will spend less money which means

00:05:35.249 --> 00:05:38.909
even less revenue for businesses which could
lead to another round of layoffs and then

00:05:38.909 --> 00:05:40.770
the cycle starts over.

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.409
Also, what about the well-off Russians?

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:47.509
I’m not talking about the billionaire oligarchs,
but the everyday millionaires that worked

00:05:47.509 --> 00:05:52.229
saved, and invested for 40 50 years just to
attain some level of financial freedom.

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:56.560
At this rate, these individuals will have
to return to work very soon.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.439
Something else to consider is that the inflation
that Russia has seen so far could just be

00:06:00.439 --> 00:06:01.729
the beginning.

00:06:01.729 --> 00:06:06.259
About 25 years ago, in 1998, Russia experienced
a similar financial fallout due to a different

00:06:06.259 --> 00:06:07.650
set of circumstances.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:12.249
Things got so bad that Russia ended up defaulting
on domestic debt which led to the Ruble jumping

00:06:12.249 --> 00:06:13.249
off a cliff.

00:06:13.249 --> 00:06:18.840
Before the crisis, you could get 1 US dollar
using 5-6 Rubles, but after the crisis, you

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:21.729
needed 30 rubles to just get a single dollar.

00:06:21.729 --> 00:06:26.840
This means that by the time things stabilized,
the Ruble had lost 80 to 90% of its value.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:31.379
If we see something similar right now, each
US dollar could be equivalent to 400 rubles

00:06:31.379 --> 00:06:32.779
by the end of this crisis.

00:06:32.779 --> 00:06:35.270
And that’s if things end up stabilizing.

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:40.699
The 1998 financial crisis was not a result
of the world powers trying to punish Russia.

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:46.099
It was due to unsustainable economic and financial
practices similar to the 2008 financial recession.

00:06:46.099 --> 00:06:51.210
So, for the most part, no one was trying to
intentionally screw over Russia, but the same

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.419
cannot be said about the current situation.

00:06:53.419 --> 00:06:58.080
So, if worse comes to worst, it’s possible
that Russia enters hyperinflation on the scale

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.169
of Venezuela, Zimbabwe, and Germany after
WW1.

00:07:01.169 --> 00:07:05.659
And at that point, there’s really nothing
left to do but start over.

00:07:05.659 --> 00:07:10.080
So, even if the Russian economy doesn’t
implode due to sanctions, it’s possible

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.779
that the economy bleeds out due to hyperinflation.

00:07:12.779 --> 00:07:18.460
STOCK MARKET SPIRAL:
The collateral damage of a financial crisis

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:23.069
and inflation alone is horrendous, but we
haven’t even discussed the future of the

00:07:23.069 --> 00:07:25.990
Russian stock market and its collateral damage.

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:30.270
At the time of making this video, Russia is
yet to reopen the stock market, but a lot

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:34.300
of Russian stocks are available on different
exchanges like the London Stock exchange.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:39.259
And so far, they have lost 33% of their value
which correlates to a $200 billion loss in

00:07:39.259 --> 00:07:40.550
market cap.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:44.349
That makes this crash the 5th worst crash
in stock market history.

00:07:44.349 --> 00:07:47.650
And that’s with the London exchange suspending
Russian stocks.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:51.029
If London hadn’t suspended these stocks,
it’s quite possible that these stocks would

00:07:51.029 --> 00:07:53.360
be down over 50% by now.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.229
For a lot of you guys, it might be confusing
as to how the stock market affects the average

00:07:57.229 --> 00:08:00.979
Russian given that the average Russian likely
doesn’t own many stocks.

00:08:00.979 --> 00:08:05.189
Well, even though the average Russian may
not incur direct losses on stock holdings,

00:08:05.189 --> 00:08:07.969
they will likely face several indirect consequences.

00:08:07.969 --> 00:08:11.780
To understand this, we have to first take
a look at why the stock market is even crashing

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:12.780
in the first place.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:15.510
The answer, in this case, is obvious.

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:19.410
With all these sanctions and restrictions,
it’s unlikely that most Russian companies

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:22.499
will be able to pull in the revenue and profits
that they used to.

00:08:22.499 --> 00:08:27.419
Many of these companies will no longer be
profitable and will be headed towards bankruptcy.

00:08:27.419 --> 00:08:31.469
And given that low investor trust in Russia,
it’s gonna be nearly impossible for these

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:34.060
companies to raise money by selling shares.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:36.450
This leaves them with only two options.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:40.030
Either they can fight for profitability until
they go bankrupt at which point they’ll

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:42.620
have to shut down operations and lay off all
their employees.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:46.590
Or, they’ll have to get a bailout from the
government which would simply increase the

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.200
odds of hyperinflation.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.430
Neither of these options is particularly favorable
to the average person.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:55.520
Either they lose their job or they lose their
savings.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:57.470
And what about retirees?

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:01.410
Most retirees in Russia depend on various
pension and retirement plans, and these plans

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.450
have investments in the stock market.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:07.220
With the stock market halving in values and
their foreign assets being frozen, it’s

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:11.190
gonna be a lot harder for these funds to pay
out their millions of beneficiaries.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:15.650
These funds may be forced to default or borrow
money to pay out their beneficiaries.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:19.580
And given that Russian interest rates are
as high as credit cards right now, you definitely

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.650
don’t want to be borrowing money.

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:26.100
As you can see, even though the average Russian
may not directly have stock holdings, a stock

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:29.820
market crash would be pretty painful for them
as well.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.990
ECONOMIC WAR:
At the end of the day, the west doesn’t

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:39.490
agree with Putin’s invasion of Ukraine,
and this has caused them to take severe action.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:44.190
100 years ago, the west may have entered into
war against Russia or entered into an arms

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:45.620
race against Russia.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:50.250
But, today, governments are trying to address
this situation diplomatically.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:54.640
Instead of fighting a deadly war or building
up weapons of mass destruction, the west is

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.090
trying to get Russia to back off by threatening
their economy.

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:02.260
And with the biggest countries and companies
on board, it seems to be pretty effective

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:03.800
in causing economic damage.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:07.980
However, it’s unclear if this will actually
lead to Russia backing down.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:12.320
Here’s the thing, even if they did back
down, Putin has permanently burned a lot of

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:14.050
bridges by deciding to invade.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:19.360
So, it’s unlikely that their economy will
just return to normal if they leave Ukraine.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:23.810
Considering this, if the Russian economy is
screwed either way, why not make a statement

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:25.280
on your way out.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:28.050
And it looks like that’s what Putin has
chosen to do.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:31.900
Looking forward, it’s unlikely that the
Russian economy will completely collapse or

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:36.380
enter hyperinflation, though those do have
non-negligible probabilities.

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:41.020
The more likely scenario is that Russia falls
down another tier amongst world powers economically

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:43.550
with the everyday Russian bearing most of
the fallout.

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:47.650
While that’s quite a shame, hopefully, all
these efforts make it substantially harder

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:50.240
for Putin to invade yet another country.

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:51.500
But only time will tell.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.940
Do you guys think the Russian economy is screwed?

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.280
Comment that down below.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.580
Also, drop a like if you feel bad for the
everyday Russian.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:02.840
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:09.120
and consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

